  "text": "To find all complex numbers \\(z\\) such that the expression \\(\\frac{z}{z+1} + \\frac{z}{z-1} + \\frac{1}{z}\\) is real, we start by simplifying the given expression. Let's write down the expression and then try to make it real.\n\nGiven expression:\n\\[\n\\frac{z}{z+1} + \\frac{z}{z-1} + \\frac{1}{z}\n\\]\n\nTo combine these fractions, we find a common denominator. The common denominator of \\(z+1\\), \\(z-1\\), and \\(z\\) is \\(z(z+1)(z-1)\\). So we rewrite each term with this common denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{z(z-1)}{z(z+1)(z-1)} + \\frac{z(z+1)}{z(z+1)(z-1)} + \\frac{(z+1)(z-1)}{z(z+1)(z-1)}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the numerators:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{z(z-1) + z(z+1) + (z+1)(z-1)}{z(z+1)(z-1)}\n\\]\n\nExpand the numerators:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{z^2 - z + z^2 + z + z^2 - 1}{z(z+1)(z-1)}\n\\]\n\nCombine like terms in the numerator:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{3z^2 - 1}{z(z+1)(z-1)}\n\\]\n\nNow, we need this expression to be real. For \\(\\frac{1}{z}\\) to be defined, \\(z \\neq 0\\). Since \\(z\\) is complex and we need \\(\\frac{1}{z}\\) to be defined (i.e., \\(z \\neq 0\\)), \\(z\\) must be non-zero. However, for the entire expression \\(\\frac{1}{z}\\) to be real and the expression as a whole to be real, we need to ensure that the imaginary part of the whole expression is zero. \n\nHere's the step-by-step reasoning in detail:\n\n1. The expression \\(\\frac{1}{z}\\) is real if \\(z\\) is purely imaginary (i.e., if \\(z = xi\\) where \\(x\\) is a real number).\n\n2. Substituting \\(z = xi\\) into the expression:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{z}{z+1} + \\frac{z}{z-1} + \\frac{1}{z} = \\frac{xi}{xi + 1} + \\frac{xi}{xi - 1} + \\frac{1}{xi}\n   \\]\n\n3. Simplify each term:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{xi}{xi + 1} = \\frac{xi}{1 + i} \\cdot \\frac{1 - i}{1 - i} = \\frac{x(i - i^2)}{1 - i^2} = \\frac{x(i + 1)}{1 + 1} = \\frac{x(i + 1)}{2} = \\frac{xi + x}{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{xi}{xi - 1} = \\frac{xi}{1 - i} \\cdot \\frac{1 + i}{1 + i} = \\frac{x(i + i^2)}{1 - i^2} = \\frac{x(i - 1)}{1 + 1} = \\frac{x(i - 1)}{2} = \\frac{xi - x}{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{xi} = -\\frac{1}{x}\n   \\]\n\n4. Combine the simplified terms:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{xi + x}{2} + \\frac{xi - x}{2} - \\frac{1}{x} = \\frac{2xi}{2} - \\frac{1}{x} = xi - \\frac{1}{x}\n   \\]\n\n5. For this expression to be real, the imaginary part must be zero:\n   \\[\n   x = 0\n   \\]\n\n6. Since \\(x = 0\\), the only complex number \\(z\\) that satisfies the condition is \\(z = 0i = 0\\).\n\nThus, the solution is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]",
